WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
Welcome to fascinating frontiers, episode 130,

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.080
coming to you on July 13th, 2026.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.100
New week, new discoveries.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:11.000
Let's see what's happening.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:13.760
A Chinese probe has reached a quasi-moon

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.820
while JWST reveals a hot Jupiter

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.300
with violently different day and night sides.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.960
JWST captured simultaneous views

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.680
of a tidally locked hot Jupiter's day side and night side

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:28.900
during a single transit event.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.060
Strong winds appear to strip water molecules apart

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.820
on the hotter face, while the cooler side

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:36.320
remains comparatively calm.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.400
The observations highlight gaps in current

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.920
atmospheric models that do not fully account

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:43.500
for such sharp gradients.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.580
Data were collected using multiple instruments

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.680
and follow-up programs will target similar worlds

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:50.880
to test the findings.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:55.600
Remember, the show covered JWST exoplanet work

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.440
in episode 127.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.480
And these new hemisphere contrasts directly address

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:04.820
open questions on biosignature detection prospects.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:07.140
Now, on a completely different note,

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:12.320
astronomers using JWST observed a tidally locked hot Jupiter

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.160
showing two dramatically different faces at once.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.780
The day side appears battered by fierce winds

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:22.120
that break apart water molecules, while the night side

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:23.060
remains calmer.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:27.240
The data exposed shortcomings in current atmospheric models

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.100
that fail to predict such sharp contrasts.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:33.060
Follow-up observations of similar planets

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.180
will test whether this pattern is common or exceptional.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.960
These results extend the JWST exoplanet program

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.980
by supplying direct evidence of violent chemistry

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.300
that earlier models overlooked.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:50.340
If Earth were locked so one side, always face the sun,

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.500
the day side would roast at temperatures hot enough

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.320
to melt lead, while the night side plunged below freezing.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.360
On a hot Jupiter orbiting its star in just a few days,

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.780
that contrast becomes extreme enough

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.660
to drive planet-wide winds faster than any hurricane on Earth.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.660
The permanent day side can exceed 2,000 degrees Celsius,

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:14.820
while the night side stays hundreds of degrees cooler,

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.480
forcing a powerful circulation cell

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.620
that carries heat across the terminator.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.320
JWST has now measured these winds indirectly

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.440
by watching how different molecules appear or vanish

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.700
between the two hemispheres during a single transit.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.380
The mechanism is simple in principle,

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:35.780
yet produces chemistry that current models

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.340
still cannot fully reproduce,

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.520
especially the apparent destruction of water on the hot side.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.980
The open question is whether these same winds

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:48.160
also makes heavier elements upward from deeper layers

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.760
or whether the atmosphere remains stratified

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.200
despite the violent flow.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.640
No one yet knows how the vertical mixing scales

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:57.740
with wind speed across the full range

00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:59.420
of observed hot Jupiters.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.040
New missions and instruments keep revealing

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.580
how little we still know about the environments

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:06.000
beyond our solar system.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.240
China's TN-2 probe has arrived at Earth's

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:14.200
quasi-satellite 469-219 Kama-Oleva,

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:15.700
following its rendezvous.

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:19.240
Mission controllers are now assessing the asteroid's

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.020
composition and rotation state

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:23.660
using onboard remote sensing instruments.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:27.680
The spacecraft carries hardware for both remote sensing

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.460
and potential sample collection.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:33.760
Data returns so far confirm the target's stable orbit

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:34.820
relative to Earth.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:38.480
Further maneuvers will refine the observation campaign

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.140
before any contact or sampling attempt.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:44.180
From extreme exoplanet atmospheres

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.140
to our own solar system's small-body exploration

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:49.940
where another spacecraft has just arrived

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:51.640
at a near-Earth asteroid.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:55.000
Astronaut Shupanshu Shukla described plans

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.940
that include an Indian space station

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:59.020
and crewed lunar missions as part

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:00.580
of the next decade's roadmap.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.220
The program builds on current robotic lunar efforts

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:07.600
and the ongoing Gaganian human space flight project.

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:10.740
Engineers are developing life support systems

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:12.820
and docking technologies required

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:15.720
for long-duration flights and station assembly.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.740
International partnerships are being explored

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:20.780
to accelerate capability development

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:22.960
in propulsion and habitat modules.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.240
The first crewed flight remains targeted

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:27.560
within the coming years.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.360
While China advances its asteroid mission,

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:34.160
India is laying out a broader human space flight roadmap

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:35.400
for the coming decade.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:39.860
King Charles III visited the UK's largest space cluster

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:42.560
on 10 July to launch the new initiative

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:44.740
designed to shape the future of the space

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:45.960
and defense economy.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:50.280
The gateway strengthens ties between the UK and ESA

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:53.460
across civil space programs and defense applications.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.040
It builds directly on existing UK facilities

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:59.780
that already support multiple ESA missions

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:02.560
in satellite technology and launch services.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.520
Officials expect the center to coordinate

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.720
future joint projects including shared infrastructure

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:11.600
for workforce development and technology transfer.

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:14.780
Formal agreements on operational scope

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:17.700
and funding contributions are the next milestones.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.040
As national agencies expand their ambitions,

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:24.040
Europe is investing in new ground infrastructure

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:27.280
to support both civil and defense space activities.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.420
The European Space Agency will establish

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:33.100
a dedicated facility in the Polish capital

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:35.740
focused on research, technology development

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:37.260
and industry partnerships.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:41.420
This marks an expansion of ESA's physical presence

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:44.880
into central Europe beyond its current member states sites.

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:47.580
Local authorities are preparing sites

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:50.580
and support structures to host the new operations.

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:54.780
Construction timelines and the initial program focus areas

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:57.840
such as satellite systems and data services

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.820
remain under discussion between ESA and Polish partners.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:06.000
The UK gateway is the latest in ESA's

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.320
growing physical footprint.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.580
A new center is now planned for central Europe.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:13.380
The company received regulatory clearance

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:15.720
to fly its initial light directing satellite

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:16.800
later this year.

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:20.120
The test craft will demonstrate controlled reflection

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.720
of sunlight towards specific ground locations.

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:25.860
Plans call for thousands of additional mirrors

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:29.220
over the next decade if the technology proves viable.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.440
Engineers must still address orbital debris

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:35.720
and brightness concerns raised during the review process.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:39.240
The first launch window opens in the coming months.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:42.520
While agencies build new facilities on the ground,

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:46.060
a commercial startup has just cleared regulatory hurdles

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:47.920
for an orbital mirror demonstration.

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:51.440
Helio has completed vibration testing

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:53.460
of its deployable antenna system

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:56.640
under a NASA phase to SBIR contract.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:59.420
The hardware targets small satellites

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:02.020
that must survive intense launch conditions.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:04.280
Performance measurements were taken

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:07.080
across several frequency ranges during the trials.

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:10.020
The successful campaign advances the design

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.460
toward an eventual orbital demonstration.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.880
Subsequent phases will examine thermal behavior

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:18.420
and full deployment sequences ahead of flight.

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:22.460
China has designated the Long March 10C variant

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:24.740
as its primary commercial launch vehicle

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:26.680
after the first booster recovery.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:29.480
The rocket employs liquid methane

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:32.900
and liquid oxygen engines to support rapid reuse.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:35.320
Additional funding has been directed

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:37.680
towards several commercial rocket developers

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:39.860
working in parallel with the state effort.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.600
Recovery procedures will be refined

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:44.400
across the next series of flights.

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:46.980
The vehicle is positioned to handle

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:49.640
both government and private sector payloads

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:50.740
in the commercial market.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:54.620
A NASA CubeSat is preparing to test new components

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:56.700
once it reaches low Earth orbit.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:00.580
The platform was chosen to keep development costs low

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:01.940
and timelines short.

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:05.400
Onboard instruments will gather data on propulsion,

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:07.860
communications, and sensor performance.

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:12.180
Launch is set for a ride share slot scheduled later this year.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:15.140
The results are expected to guide designs

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:16.920
for later operational missions.

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:22.200
The young open cluster M29 sits just under 8 degrees south

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:26.120
of Gamma Seigne and is already prominent after sunset.

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:29.220
Binoculars or small telescopes can reveal it

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:31.180
even under moderate light pollution.

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:33.960
Several bright blue stars stand out

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:36.100
against the surrounding Milky Way field.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:39.600
Its distance and age provide a useful reference

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:41.740
for studies of recent star formation.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:45.540
Viewing conditions stay favorable through the coming week.

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:47.920
From emerging commercial concepts

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:49.940
to proven long-duration missions,

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:53.600
the Voyager-1 spacecraft continues to send data

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:55.600
from the edge of interstellar space.

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:57.800
After nearly 49 years,

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:02.220
the Voyager-1 spacecraft has reached only 166 AU,

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:06.260
roughly 24.8 billion kilometers from Earth.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:10.340
The distance underscores how vast interstellar space remains

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:12.120
even at record speeds.

00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:15.680
Both Voyager probes continue to return measurements

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:17.240
from the Heliopause region.

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:19.360
Power levels are declining,

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.600
but instruments are still operational.

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:26.160
Scientists expect data collection to continue into the 2030s.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:30.600
A kilometer deep instrument in Japan recorded a possible first signal

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:32.480
from the cumulative neutrino flux

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:34.960
of all past supernovae.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:38.260
Confirmation would provide a new census of stellar deaths

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:39.680
across cosmic time.

00:09:40.340 --> 00:09:43.340
The detector sensitivity allows it to register events

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:47.320
far below the threshold of individual supernova observations.

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:51.000
Analysis is ongoing to distinguish the signal

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.180
from background noise.

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:55.720
Additional data runs are planned through the coming year.

00:09:56.360 --> 00:09:59.920
Most lunar water is thought to exist in chemically bound form

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:03.020
within the mantle rather than as ice at the poles.

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:06.680
Polar crater deposits represent only a small fraction

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:07.880
of the total inventory.

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:11.500
Sample analysis and remote sensing both point

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:14.560
to an interior source that has not fully outgassed.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:18.100
Future landers may drill or use seismic methods

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:19.860
to measure the deeper reservoir.

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:25.100
LIGO, Virgo, and Kegra have now catalogued hundreds of mergers

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:27.040
revealing distinct populations.

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:31.360
Some events match predictions for isolated binary evolution

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:34.820
while others suggest dynamical formation in dense clusters.

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:38.120
The growing sample allows statisticians

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:40.560
to separate the two channels for the first time.

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:43.620
Continued observations will tighten constraints

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:46.200
on progenitor masses and merger rates.

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:50.300
Next observing runs aim to double the current event count.

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:53.220
The space telescope identified quasars

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:56.140
at greater distances than any previously recorded.

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:59.380
Their existence at such early cosmic times

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:02.360
challenges models of how supermassive black holes

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:03.460
grew so quickly.

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:07.200
Spectra confirmed the objects are powered by accretion

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:08.980
onto massive central engines.

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:12.380
The discoveries add new data points to the timeline

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:13.740
of reionization.

00:11:14.620 --> 00:11:17.320
Additional candidates from the survey are under review.

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:20.300
Next time we'll be watching for confirmation

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:23.120
on the supernova neutrino background signal

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:24.600
from the Japanese detector.

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:27.360
That's fascinating frontiers for today.

